---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { site, description, pageTitle } from '../../content';
import { appBySlug } from '../../data/apps';

const app = appBySlug('tinyemail')!;
const path = '/apps/tinyemail';
const title = pageTitle(site, 'apps/tinyemail');
const desc = description(site, 'apps/tinyemail');

const faqs = [
  {
    "question": "Is tinyEmail only for new stores?",
    "answer": "Evaluate the required campaigns, segments and automations regardless of store age or order count. There is no universal graduation date."
  },
  {
    "question": "What Shopify automations are documented?",
    "answer": "The supplier guide documents purchase and abandoned-cart templates. Check the connected account for current templates, available controls and the behavior of your exact journey."
  },
  {
    "question": "Does Shopify synchronization prove email permission?",
    "answer": "No. Customer existence and synchronization are separate from permission for the intended marketing channel and purpose. Test actual recipient eligibility and suppression."
  },
  {
    "question": "Can I use product content in messages?",
    "answer": "The automation guide describes brand and order content populating templates. Preview the actual product images, text, prices and destinations required by your campaign."
  },
  {
    "question": "How should I compare Sequenzy?",
    "answer": "Demonstrate the same required workflow and compare total operating cost. Move only if the practical benefit covers migration and maintenance; order count alone is not a reason."
  },
  {
    "question": "How much does tinyEmail cost?",
    "answer": "Obtain current account terms for audience, send volume, required features and overages. Historical entry prices and assumed free allowances do not establish your bill."
  },
  {
    "question": "Does this review establish SMS availability?",
    "answer": "No. This review evaluates the documented email workflow. Confirm any additional channel requirement with a supported supplier rather than assuming inclusion or prescribing another app."
  },
  {
    "question": "How do I avoid duplicate cart messages?",
    "answer": "Assign one sender and queue owner per journey and cohort. Stop old entries, cancel or drain pending messages and verify purchase suppression, current permission and replay deduplication before replacement activation."
  },
  {
    "question": "Are campaign results promised?",
    "answer": "No. The fixtures are proposed checks and the contribution example uses hypothetical inputs. Measure incremental contribution separately from attributed orders."
  }
];
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
  <section class="container py-16">
    <a class="link" href="/apps">← All app reviews</a>
    <p class="editorial-kicker mt-6">Supplier scope checked November 25, 2026</p>
    <h1 class="hero-title mt-5">{app.name} review for Shopify stores</h1>
    <p class="mt-7 max-w-3xl text-xl leading-9">Evaluate a separate email workspace for Shopify campaigns, catalog content, audiences and documented automations. Suitability depends on your actual workflow and operating cost, with no preset order-count or month-based graduation rule.</p>
  </section>
  <section class="container pb-12">
    <div class="prose-shopify mb-12">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-4xl font-black">Campaign workspace and documented scope</h2>
      <p>tinyEmail provides a separate email workspace connected to Shopify. Its <a href="https://support.tinyemail.com/portal/en/kb/articles/integrate-shopify-with-tinyemail-21-5-2024-1">integration guide</a> describes account connection and customer synchronization. Confirm the connection and account eligibility during evaluation; the guide identifies an account-type restriction, so installation alone should not be treated as entitlement.</p>
      <p>The <a href="https://www.tinyemail.com/features/audience">audience page</a> presents segmentation and audience management. The <a href="https://support.tinyemail.com/portal/en/kb/articles/how-to-set-up-email-automation-for-shopify-21-5-2024-1">Shopify automation guide</a> documents purchase and abandoned-cart templates populated with brand and order information. These support a real campaign and automation evaluation, without assuming every branch, trigger or editor option is available in every plan.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify mb-12">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-4xl font-black">Choose by work required, not a graduation clock</h2>
      <p>A separate workspace can help an operator organize creative, product content and audiences. AI assistance can provide a first draft, but the useful question is whether the team can review and send an accurate campaign with acceptable effort. Time the task in your own evaluation rather than relying on a promised first-hour launch.</p>
      <p>There is no supported rule that a store outgrows tinyEmail at 150 orders, month four or within a year. Map actual required journeys and audience conditions, demonstrate them, then compare the cost of staying with the cost of changing. A larger store with straightforward needs may retain it; a small store with a demanding data model may need a different solution.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify mb-12">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-4xl font-black">Proposed catalog campaign fixture</h2>
      <p>Prepare a candle launch with one available product, one sold-out variant and a recently changed price. Select the intended products, edit the campaign and preview on desktop and mobile. Verify image, currency, variant destination and offer dates against Shopify immediately before sending. Decide what to do if stock changes after scheduling.</p>
      <p>Give the AI draft factual constraints: ingredients, burn-time evidence, sale dates and exclusions. Review every product claim and link, remove invented guarantees and check the plain-text version. Record the time spent selecting products and correcting copy; compare this effort against your existing workflow using the same brief.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify mb-12">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-4xl font-black">Recipient acceptance: synced is not subscribed</h2>
      <p>Create staff fixtures for an opted-in subscriber, a customer without promotional permission, an unsubscribed address and a duplicate imported address. Compare synchronization counts with the actual eligible recipient preview. Store channel, purpose, source and permission evidence separately from customer existence.</p>
      <p>Check exclusions again after a scheduled campaign audience changes. A segment preview captured yesterday is not proof that a newly unsubscribed customer will be excluded today. Investigate discrepancies before sending and document whether the provider resolves them automatically or the operator needs a supported procedure.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify mb-12">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-4xl font-black">Segments: test inclusion and exclusion</h2>
      <p>For a proposed replenishment campaign, specify the exact product and purchase window, then make boundary fixtures: just inside the window, just outside, refunded purchase and a second recent purchase. Compare the resulting members to a small expected-recipient table. Use only conditions the connected account actually exposes.</p>
      <p>Repeat after updating a source field and record refresh delay. If a desired condition is unavailable, estimate the effort and reliability of a supported alternative before declaring a migration necessary. Avoid labeling the entire product incapable of segmentation because one particular workflow has not been demonstrated.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify mb-12">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-4xl font-black">Automation entry and pending-message fixtures</h2>
      <p>For each selected template, record the actual entry event, delay, content, exit condition and owner. Use staff orders to verify product rendering and expected timing. The vendor guide describes templates; it does not establish that your purchase-suppression or custom branching requirement will work without validation.</p>
      <p>Rehearse a purchase after cart entry, an unsubscribe while a message is queued, a repeated event and a second cart from the same customer. Acceptance requires the intended eligible message once, with current purchase and suppression state respected. If a case cannot be demonstrated, hold that journey and ask for a supported solution rather than sending live traffic to discover the behavior.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify mb-12">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-4xl font-black">Native Shopify comparison and one-owner cutover</h2>
      <p>Shopify Messaging, formerly Shopify Email, has native campaigns and automations. Compare the exact catalog, audience and journey task before adding a separate workspace. Existing workflows may already meet the requirement. The linked native review contains legacy graduation language; this page does not adopt its universal migration prescription.</p>
      <p>If switching a specific flow, inventory its pending queue and stop new enrollments in the retiring sender. Cancel or drain pending messages under a documented plan, then activate the replacement for a mutually exclusive cohort. Recheck permission and purchases at send time and deduplicate replayed events. Keeping a founder newsletter elsewhere requires separate audience and frequency coordination, not an automatic two-ESP recommendation.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify mb-12">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-4xl font-black">Pricing worksheet instead of an entry-price promise</h2>
      <p>Request current terms for your account type, billable audience, monthly sends, required automation features, overages and support. Model a regular month and a seasonal peak using the same inputs for tinyEmail, native Shopify and any alternative. Do not infer a permanent free plan or exact bill from historical prices.</p>
      <p>Include creative review hours, segment maintenance, DNS setup and migration effort. Compare Sequenzy or Omnisend only where their demonstrated workflow addresses an identified need. A lower headline fee is not a reason to move if the integration and operator workload increase the total cost.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify mb-12">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-4xl font-black">Illustrative campaign economics</h2>
      <p>Assume a controlled comparison estimates 12 incremental orders at $50 each and 50% contribution before email costs. That is $300 contribution. With hypothetical $60 platform cost and $120 labor, the balance is $120. These assumptions are not vendor prices or predicted results. With zero incremental orders, those costs yield a $180 loss.</p>
      <p>Report attributed orders separately from estimated incremental orders. Count unique orders across campaign and automation reports, exclude refunds consistently and document the comparison period. An open rate or recovery percentage alone does not establish profit, and no category-wide minimum recovery rate is prescribed here.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify mb-12">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-4xl font-black">Rollout, rollback and ongoing review</h2>
      <p>Complete catalog and staff-recipient fixtures before a bounded live campaign. Authenticate the sending domain using current provider instructions and verify test delivery without removing records used by other senders. Review actual mobile rendering, tracked destinations, footer and unsubscribe handling before expanding.</p>
      <p>If product data or recipient selection is wrong, pause scheduled sends and new flow entries. Identify pending messages before resuming. Keep the last known audience rules, content version and ownership record so the next operator can restore a verified state without accidentally replaying a journey.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify mb-12">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-4xl font-black">Keep, improve or replace</h2>
      <p>Review mismatched recipients, broken product links and automation exceptions after each material catalog or workflow change. Keep a small regression fixture set for the next sale. A recurring operator check is more useful than a calendar deadline to abandon the platform.</p>
      <p>Stay when required campaigns and journeys pass, the team can maintain them and total cost is acceptable. Improve configuration where a supported fix resolves a gap. Compare replacements when a documented requirement remains unmet or measured operating economics favor a move. No unsupported merchant success story, order threshold or mandatory graduation stack is needed for that decision.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card mb-12">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-3xl font-black">Related decisions</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><a class="link" href="/apps/shopify-email">Native Shopify review</a></li>
        <li><a class="link" href="/compare/sequenzy-vs-shopify-email">Separate sender comparison</a></li>
        <li><a class="link" href="/apps/sequenzy">Sequenzy review</a></li>
        <li><a class="link" href="/guides/pricing-models">Pricing models</a></li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>
  <FaqSection faqs={faqs} title="tinyEmail Shopify FAQ" />
</BaseLayout>
